 \begin{abstract}
-Prediction disagreement is widely used as reliability evidence, yet common error can cancel from a prediction difference. We study this failure in deterministic MRI inter-slice interpolation, where the physical anchors supporting one missing target can be controlled. Across 24 fastMRI brain examinations, 115 IXI brain examinations, and eight patient clusters from NingXia knee MRI, target-preserving disjoint contexts improve error ranking over dependence-preserving controls by $\Delta\rho=.092/.095/.193$. Under clean sharing, only 2.61/1.58/0.25\% of paired-error energy remains observable. A prospectively frozen $\times4$ test spans HAT, SwinIR, I3Net, an adapted SAINT-style network, and an internal CycleINR-style model. Every architecture has positive disjoint-minus-shared observable gain on fastMRI (8.0--22.6 points) and an independent IXI replication (25.2--30.2 points); all 115 IXI case effects per model are positive. Examination-ranking gains remain cohort dependent. An exact analytical ladder also falsifies anchor count as a monotone explanation: local estimator influence is more informative, but its finite-response approximation is not stable or superior for every architecture. A 125-case external MR angiography experiment tests acquired-only score support with fixed analytical estimators. Retrospective benefit audits retain a degraded-model exception and expose sensitivity to score--endpoint scale agreement. Exploratory controls retain local gains under simultaneous inference but weaken ranking and benefit claims. The contribution is a bounded error-geometry mechanism, not calibrated uncertainty or prospective acquisition benefit.
+When does prediction agreement cease to be evidence of reliability? Two reconstructions can agree because the same acquired measurements influence them similarly, aligning errors that disappear from their difference. We introduce the Measurement-Influence Reliability Audit, a framework that combines same-target measurement interventions, error-energy decomposition, and local estimator-response analysis without retraining a reconstruction network. A local covariance proposition specifies when shared acquisition variation suppresses disagreement, and motivates an influence statistic that accounts for the strength and direction of responses to physical anchors. Retrospective experiments across three MRI cohorts and five frozen architecture implementations support a reproducible loss of local error observability under sharing; clean shared controls hide approximately 97--99.8\% of paired-error energy in the original two-architecture experiment. Influence refines overlap count where the probe is stable, while fixed-count and architecture failures delimit this interpretation. Case-level ranking is less consistent, and a limited acquired-only scoring experiment does not establish a superior reliability score. The framework exposes the distinction between observable disagreement and hidden common error: agreement alone cannot certify correctness under shared influential evidence.
 \end{abstract}
 
 \section{Introduction}
-Sparse through-plane MRI is routinely densified by interpolation or learned slice synthesis. A reconstruction can look anatomically plausible while smoothing, displacing, or inventing structure, so a reliability signal is desirable when dense targets are absent. Ensembles, test-time transformations, and cross-context consistency commonly use output variation as such a signal \cite{lakshminarayanan2017ensembles,gal2016dropout,edupuganti2021uncertainty}. Their appeal is intuitive: if plausible predictors disagree, at least one deserves scrutiny. Their blind spot is equally fundamental but less often controlled experimentally: predictors can agree because they reuse the same influential information and therefore make aligned errors.
+Prediction agreement is often treated as evidence of reliability. It is more informative when alternative predictions fail differently and less informative when they inherit the same error. Our question is: \emph{when does prediction agreement cease to be evidence of reliability?} We study deterministic cross-context disagreement, not all Bayesian, probabilistic, or calibrated uncertainty methods.
 
-We turn this abstract dependence problem into a physical intervention. For one missing MRI slice, phase-shifted acquisition grids can provide reconstruction contexts that preserve the target while changing which acquired slice anchors support it. This makes it possible to ask not merely whether outputs differ, but whether dependence among their generating measurements determines which target-error components disagreement can observe.
-
-Figure~\ref{fig:mechanism} states the proposed chain. For predictions $f_a=y+e_a$ and $f_b=y+e_b$, output separation observes $e_a-e_b$. Shared measurements can induce similar local estimator responses; similar responses can align $e_a$ and $e_b$; aligned errors cancel in the difference. The elementary identity is not our novelty. The scientific question is what determines the error inner product. Our evidence identifies shared local measurement influence as one determinant in MRI interpolation.
+MRI slice interpolation offers a controlled setting for this question. A retrospectively available dense examination identifies the missing target plane, the acquired slice positions, and the physical identity of each anchor. Two contexts can reconstruct the same target while sharing or separating acquired measurements. This lets us intervene on evidence dependence rather than merely compare networks with different architectures or accuracy.
 
