-\subsection{The local mechanism is stronger than a global ranking law}
-The arrow-wise analysis supports the local chain in all six strata. Influence-to-error-alignment partial $\rho$ is .219--.410; alignment-to-normalized-disagreement is $-.933$ to $-.889$; influence-to-observable-fraction is $-.412$ to $-.211$. All 18 intervals exclude zero. The context-level consequence is weaker: greater alignment predicts poorer disagreement--error ranking in all strata, but intervals exclude zero in only three of six. A local pair can have aligned errors because common anchors influence both predictions, while the global case-ranking effect also depends on anatomy, error magnitude, and the small set of contexts. The cancellation arrow is constrained by the energy identity; its correlation is not independent mechanistic validation. The empirical evidence is the controlled change in error geometry, not causal mediation.
