-The ranking arrow does not generalize uniformly across cohorts. On IXI, $\Delta\rho$ is .031/.035/.025/.032/.134 for HAT/SwinIR/I3Net/SAINT/CycleINR and every interval is positive. On fastMRI, only CycleINR is clearly positive (.342 [.113,.711]); the other four intervals cross zero. The frozen stable-wrong rule also yields 4/120 shared fastMRI CycleINR planes and zero for every other shared model and every disjoint model. An exploratory 10,000-bootstrap reanalysis keeps local gains positive for every model under all four within-/between-case mean/median combinations. Five-model simultaneous ranking intervals are positive for only HAT, SwinIR, and SAINT on IXI and none on fastMRI. Local geometry is more robust than ordering.
+The weaker downstream result is examination ordering. Under simultaneous inference, ranking gains exclude zero for HAT, SwinIR and SAINT on IXI, but not I3Net/CycleINR and not any fastMRI model. A local mechanism can be reproducible while a global ranking law fails. The historical shared-threshold stable-wrong rule identifies only four of 120 shared CycleINR fastMRI planes and none for the other models; those thresholds came from the reported test distribution, not external detector validation.
 
-The influence probe also identifies a boundary rather than a clean five-model replication. SAINT passes all amplitude/sign gates (minimum $\rho=.940$); its broad influence association is .706 [.680,.734] with error cosine and $-.698$ [$-.730$,$-.666$] with observable fraction. Yet influence exceeds count for error cosine by only .014 [$-.002$,.029], and its exact-one-overlap, geometry/examination-adjusted estimate is .063 [$-.021$,.149]. I3Net and CycleINR fail the prospectively frozen stability gate and are classified as phenomenon-only. Hence stable non-transformer influence is directionally consistent, but ``influence beats count across all architectures'' is falsified.
+\paragraph{Practical uncertainty constructions.}
+The shared neural control itself is a two-view TTA consistency construction. A new, acquired-mask experiment additionally tests a two-member Linear/Cubic ensemble on 115 IXI examinations. Changing only the companion phase improves fixed-reference-error ranking for ensemble variance by .031 [.012,.050] and .036 [.016,.056] under two-direction simultaneous intervals; local gains are about 32.1 points. Disagreement MAE is reported alongside it. Population variance equals the squared pair difference divided by four, so these are related proxies, not two independent discoveries. The same complete-support protocol is infeasible in shallow fastMRI volumes and they are excluded by geometry. This extends the measurement test to a practical analytic ensemble, not to all neural ensembles or calibrated uncertainty methods.
 
-Architecture diversity is not synonymous with independent failure. At fastMRI $\times4$, the median examination error-rank correlation among the prospectively frozen five models is .981, and six of 24 cases are in the top error quartile for all five. An exploratory full-bank audit in the supplement expands this to seven cohort--factor strata, 6--13 recovered models per stratum, and 483 pairs: every stratum's median case-error rank correlation exceeds .93. Cross-family pairing improves disagreement--error ranking for some implicit targets but is neutral or adverse for others. Pixelwise error cosine is lower for CycleINR--I3Net (.564 median) than HAT--SwinIR (.918), showing that local error diversity can increase even while case difficulty remains shared.
+\subsection{A bounded attempt at actionable scoring}
+We test one acquired-only guard against small-disagreement failures: $G=\max\{F_{\rm dev}(D_n),F_{\rm dev}(A_n)\}$, where $A$ is Anchor-CV, $n$ denotes acquired-range normalization, and each empirical CDF is fitted on 12 development fastMRI cases. It adds an acquired-data floor when predictors reuse phase-0 anchors; it is not a learned influence score. Two fixed model pairs cover HAT/SwinIR and I3Net/CycleINR. Thresholds and the single formula are then applied to the other 12 fastMRI cases and all 115 IXI cases, compared with raw normalized disagreement, adjacent MAE/RMSE, Anchor-CV, range and spacing.
 
-Table~\ref{tab:p15-master} integrates all 13 master-table baselines on all three $\times4$ cohorts; the supplement retains all five metrics over 111 configurations, including 52 multi-scale and 20 extended-budget rows. Native full-plane metrics remain distinct from masked QA endpoints. Across all 13 models, median case-error rank correlation is .997/.899/1.000 on IXI/fastMRI/NingXia (78 pairs each). Budget matters: NingXia Res-SRDiff $\times2$ MAE falls from 440.61 to 140.45/118.25 at 60/90 epochs, yet remains above Linear's 78.53. Fifteen of 20 MAE budget comparisons favor longer training under simultaneous inference; five are inconclusive. Thus the short-budget negative run does not establish an intrinsic family failure.
+Neither result supports a new method claim. The fixed both-high-error/low-disagreement label has no positive internal-evaluation cases and only two per pair on IXI. Internal AUC/AP are therefore undefined, and all 12 IXI guard-minus-baseline simultaneous AP intervals include zero. Historical cohort reuse further prevents an untouched-holdout interpretation. We stop after this candidate. The mechanism is reproducible within its scope, while a universally superior scalar reliability score remains open. Full values, degenerate resamples and earlier failed candidates are retained in the supplement.
 
