-\section{Discussion}
-The central finding is that physical measurement dependence changes which paired-error components survive in disagreement. MRI interpolation makes this testable because target position and physical anchor identity are known. When contexts depend strongly on the same measurements, their local responses and target errors align, causing disagreement to understate error. Measurement-disjoint contexts expose a larger error component.
-
-The progression from overlap count to influence is scientifically important. Count is attractive because it is exact and acquisition-only, but the zero-to-one reversal falsifies a strict dose law. Unequal coefficient mass explains analytical configurations, and paired neural finite responses transfer the idea to nonlinear interpolators. Influence is local to a target--context--input configuration, not an intrinsic global model coefficient. The result also clarifies what is and is not actionable. Anchor-CV offers an inexpensive acquired-only examination signal and tracks same-model retrospective densification value across most evaluated fastMRI and NingXia models. Yet adjacent differences and candidate-bank disagreement carry similar rank information, Res-SRDiff reverses the NingXia direction, and none of these examination scores localizes the worst physical plane. The mechanism therefore informs reliability-context design and triage experiments without implying calibrated uncertainty, autonomous reacquisition, or a universally superior scalar.
-
-Several limitations are material. NingXia contains 22 series but only eight independent patients; its fixed-count result is null/mixed. Architecture breadth now covers two brain cohorts, but the five-architecture intervention remains limited to brain T2 and has no knee replication. External MRA extends only the analytical test. The local SAINT and CycleINR-style implementations are adapted/internal reproductions, not exact official models. Only SAINT passes all new-architecture influence-stability gates; its beyond-count estimate is imprecise. I3Net/CycleINR support the physical-dependence phenomenon but not a finite-response influence claim. The grids have different feasible overlap support, the final ranking arrow remains cohort dependent, and finite differences cannot identify a global Jacobian. Densification remains retrospective and compares factor-specific checkpoints. IXI adds one same-architecture pair, not a broad matrix. Scanner time, motion, and clinical cost are absent; target-derived output masks require separate deployment validation. A train/validation patient overlap affects historical checkpoint-selection provenance, although test patients are disjoint. No result establishes diagnostic safety.
+The complete 13-model reconstruction benchmark, training-budget audit, mechanism-chain associations, retrospective densification, normalization variants, and external analytical MRA studies remain in the supplement. They contextualize scope rather than establish a universal method. In particular, current-error adjustment weakens Anchor-CV benefit associations, Res-SRDiff retains an adverse boundary, and anatomy-specific ranking need not improve general error ranking. No clinical, prospective acquisition, or natural-image generalization claim follows from this work.
 
 \section{Conclusion}
-Shared error can make deterministic MRI interpolators agree while jointly wrong. Across three cohorts and five neural architectures, same-target interventions support a local measurement-dependence mechanism, with cohort-dependent ranking and architecture-dependent influence stability. Retrospective benefit associations weaken after current-error adjustment or scale changes. Prospective acquisition and endpoint validation remain necessary.
+Agreement between frozen reconstructions can conceal common error inherited from shared influential measurements. A same-target audit, a conditional covariance model, and replication with explicit failures make that limitation measurable. Local observability is more reproducible than examination ranking, and practical target-free superiority remains unproven.
 
